  "turn": {
    "pericope": "Matthew 13:31-33, 44-52",
    "refKey": "Matt.13.31-Matt.13.52",
    "gravity": "Solomon, offered anything at all, asks not for riches but for an understanding heart; Paul declares that nothing can separate us from the love of God; and Jesus piles up images of a kingdom worth selling everything to own. The nerve: what is the one thing you would trade all the others for — and have you found it?",
    "trap": "The five small parables get flattened into \"the kingdom is great, so try hard\" — losing both the joy (the man sells all gladly, not grimly) and the realism (the net holds bad fish too). Or only the comfortable images get preached and the net's hard sorting quietly drops out.",
    "hinge": "The treasure costs everything and is stumbled on by accident, bought with joy; the leaven is hidden and tiny yet takes over the whole; the net gathers everyone yet sorts at the shore. Free gift and total cost, smallest and total, gathered and sifted — all at once.",
    "doors": [
      {
        "landing": "Hidden leaven",
        "claim": "The kingdom starts as the smallest, most hidden thing and works silently through the whole — so do not despise small beginnings, and let grace leaven all of you.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Augustine",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The leaven signifies love, because it causes activity and fermentation; the love implanted in us ought to grow until it changes the whole soul into its own perfection."
          },
          {
            "author": "Hilary of Poitiers",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The Lord compares himself to leaven, for leaven communicates the power it has received to a whole heap of its own kind."
          }
        ],
        "say": "What God has hidden in you is small and quiet now, but it is meant to work through the whole of your life.",
        "do": "Point to one hidden, unglamorous beginning of grace in them, and have them let it spread to one more corner this week."
      },
      {
        "landing": "Sell all with joy",
        "claim": "The one who finds the treasure and the pearl sells everything — not grimly but gladly — because next to the one thing, everything else is worth letting go.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Gregory the Great",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The pearl of great price is the sweetness of the heavenly kingdom, which he who has found it sells all and buys."
          },
          {
            "author": "Jerome",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "He hides the treasure not out of envy, but as one who treasures up what he would not lose — he hides in his heart the thing he prizes."
          }
        ],
        "say": "When you have really seen the treasure, letting the rest go stops feeling like loss and starts feeling like joy.",
        "do": "Name one thing they are clutching that is keeping their hands too full to take the pearl — and invite them to sell it, gladly."
      },
      {
        "landing": "The net and the shore",
        "claim": "The net gathers every kind of fish, and the sorting comes only at the shore — so being inside the catch is not yet being safe; do not presume.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Jerome",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "When the net is drawn to the shore, then will be shown the true test for separating the fishes."
          },
          {
            "author": "John Chrysostom",
            "tradition": "greek-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "Lest we trust in preaching only, or think faith enough by itself without a life, he adds the net: good and bad are gathered, and parted only at the end."
          }
        ],
        "say": "You are in the net — gathered, named, carried — and the net is not yet the shore; live like it.",
        "do": "Turn presumption into watchfulness: one way the hearer has mistaken being gathered for being finished."
      }
    ],
    "synthesis": {
      "convergence": "Each text turns on valuing the one thing: Solomon asking for an understanding heart instead of riches (1 Kings 3), the psalmist prizing God's word above gold (Psalm 119), Paul certain that nothing is worth being cut off from God's love (Romans 8), and a kingdom worth selling everything to own (the parables).",
      "claim": "There is one thing worth more than all the rest, and it has already found you — sell everything, and keep it gladly.",
      "here": "For people whose lives are cluttered with many goods and many worries, the move is to name the single treasure and let everything else find its true size — resting, with Paul, in the truth that the treasure (God's love) is holding them, not the other way around."
    },
    "subtract": "Three doors, three landings — transformation (hidden leaven), joyful surrender (sell all), sober self-examination (the net and the shore). Choose one and let the others go.",
